What is Dependency Injection (DI)?
Dependency Injection means giving (or injecting) the things a class needs instead of having the class create them itself.

Think of it like this:
Instead of a chef (your class) going out to buy ingredients (dependencies), you have someone else bring them to the kitchen already prepared.

Why Use DI in Unity?
Unity is component-based, and you often have scripts that depend on other scripts or systems (like GameManager, AudioManager, Inventory, etc.).
Using DI frameworks (like Zenject, Extenject, or Unity’s built-in Service Locator) helps manage those dependencies automatically.

Benefits of DI in Unity
Cleaner Code — Your classes don’t need to find objects with FindObjectOfType or GetComponent.

Better Performance — Avoids repeated FindObjectOfType, GetComponent, or Resources.Load calls.
These Unity calls are expensive, especially in Update() or during scene loads.
DI ensures dependencies are set once at startup.

Easier Testing — You can inject mock objects for testing.

More Flexible Architecture — You can easily replace systems (e.g., AudioManager → AdvancedAudioManager) without touching other scripts.

When to Use DI in Unity
Use DI when:
- Your project starts getting complex (many managers, services, or systems).
- You find yourself doing lots of FindObjectOfType, GetComponent, or Singleton  patterns.
- You want to test or reuse code outside of Unity’s scene system.

In Short
What:
Give a class its dependencies instead of making it create them

Why:
Cleaner, faster, testable, flexible code

When:
As your Unity project grows and you have many interconnected systems

